343	  /* Rolling digits (RollingDigitsText) — 2D cash-register snap, not 3D.
344	   *
345	   * Timing is on the motion scale, all on the `enter` curve. The ROLL — a
346	   * wheel already on screen translating to its new digit — runs over `base`.
347	   * `stagger` defaults to zero so every wheel starts and lands together, one
348	   * snap for the whole figure; it stays a token so a consumer can dial in a
349	   * right-to-left cascade (ones place leads) without patching the package.
350	   *
351	   * A whole SLOT — a wheel or a grouping separator — opens from zero width
352	   * over `slow` and collapses to zero width over `base`, fading as it goes.
353	   * Width and opacity are the same animation, and it runs CONCURRENTLY with
354	   * the roll of the wheels already on screen. `opacity-ratio` is the FRACTION
355	   * of that duration the fade occupies: opacity finishes early so an arriving
356	   * glyph (which is drawn at full 1ch and overflows its narrower slot) is
357	   * already opaque by the time it stops overlapping its neighbour.
358	   *
359	   * `digit-width` and `separator-width` are the two slot widths. Both have to
360	   * be fixed lengths because `width` cannot animate from `auto`, and both are
361	   * relative units so the figure scales with its type.
362	   *
363	   * `digit-width` defaults to 1ch, which is the advance of "0" — very close to,
364	   * but not exactly, the TABULAR advance the figure actually renders at (Google
365	   * Sans Flex: 1ch is about 2% under). Every wheel is centred in its own slot,
366	   * so the error is symmetric per digit rather than cumulative, and the only
367	   * visible effect is a figure fractionally tighter than the same string set as
368	   * plain text. Retune this if a consumer's face drifts further.
369	   *
370	   * `separator-width` is measured to the default face's comma. Too narrow only
371	   * means the glyph is optically tight — the separator does not clip, because
372	   * its overflow is visible and its glyph is centred.
373	   *
374	   * `decimals-size` / `decimals-rise` / `decimals-gap` are all in em of the
375	   * PARENT — the integer part — not of the decimals. That is what keeps the
376	   * decimals a fixed PROPORTION of the figure at every size: a role passed as
377	   * `smallDecimalsComponent` carries its own absolute font size (LabelText is a
378	   * flat 12px), which would read correctly beside 23px integers and far too
379	   * small beside 40px ones. So these tokens own the size and the passed role
380	   * owns family, weight and tracking — applied by the
381	   * `.ds-rolling-digits-decimals > *` rule in index.css, which sizes the CHILD
382	   * precisely so the wrapper keeps inheriting the parent's em and `rise`/`gap`
383	   * stay measured against it. Override `decimals-size` to change the
384	   * proportion; `decimals-rise` needs re-tuning alongside it, since top-aligning
385	   * a taller decimals box takes less rise. */
386	  --ui-rolling-digits-stagger: 0ms;
387	  --ui-rolling-digits-opacity-ratio: 0.55;
388	  --ui-rolling-digits-digit-width: 1ch;
389	  --ui-rolling-digits-separator-width: 0.34em;
390	  --ui-rolling-digits-decimals-size: 0.5em;
391	  --ui-rolling-digits-decimals-rise: 0.525em;
392	  --ui-rolling-digits-decimals-gap: 0.06em;
